It was permitted to hear the groaning of the dying in neighboring houses), and you forbade the celestial shield-bearer, who had struck down the people on every side and inflicted death-dealing blows everywhere, from harming any of ours from among the household, or aiming his gleaming sword at our limbs. A thousand fell at my right hand, and a thousand at my left, yet I myself was safe; and (which you will wonder at more) I went to the houses of the sick, not refusing to treat them with my hands (and yet I felt no contagion of the plague), I breathed in the foul stenches and filthy vapors often with impunity, unharmed. Oh, how great was this favor! How great and how divine was this love! It was pleasing to remember the holy eulogy in which Christ gave his beloved student the power to tread upon twisted snakes, to drink lurid poisons, and to overcome all kinds of enemies. This most sharp Lion of the sacred tribe of Judah, who led the ancient serpent and the gates of the <term>Barathrum</term> <gloss>the abyss/Hell</gloss> in triumph—he, I say, the great savior of the world, Jesus—granted these things most truly to me (if to anyone). You established my boat safe on the shore while it was crossing the middle of <term>Scylla</term> <gloss>a sea monster</gloss> and the cruel <term>Charybdis</term> <gloss>a whirlpool</gloss>. I saw many shipwrecked crowds (a miserable spectacle) drowned in rapid whirlpools and tossed through the waves; through various mishaps, through a thousand dangers threatening human life, you brought me safe and sheltered. You will always be my portion, and my shield, and my salvation; you are my fortified tower and unassailable asylum. Great indeed are the things mentioned above, but greater things follow;

<summary>The author describes surviving the plague while tending to the sick, attributing his immunity to the protection of Christ, whom he identifies as the Lion of Judah.</summary>
<keywords>Plague, Christ, Lion of Judah, Miraculous Protection, Barathrum</keywords>
